//! GENERATED by `cartalith-native/tools/um_block2_capture.js` -- do not hand-edit.
//!
//! Golden parity for `urban_adapter.rs`'s **block-2** `_um*` adapter --
//! `URBAN_MORPHOLOGY_SCOPE.md` milestone 17a, the row `OUTSTANDING_WORK.md`
//! §2.1 recorded as blocked on "no harness can run `_um*` inside the host's
//! full civ scope".
//!
//! **That blocker was wrong.** All four of the reference's `<script>` blocks
//! evaluate in a bare `vm` context, in the browser's own order, given one
//! self-similar Proxy standing in for the DOM. The harness's header records
//! the measurement and the three `um_capture.js` conventions that could not
//! carry over; the one worth repeating here is the `let` trap:
//! `ctx.GW = 96` is invisible to block-2 code, because `GW` is a global
//! **lexical** binding and not a property of the `vm` context. Every such
//! global is assigned by running an assignment *inside* the context, and the
//! harness re-reads each one from inside afterwards before capturing anything.
//!
//! # What is fixture and what is under test
//!
//! Fed in -- these are the Rust functions' own explicit parameters, so feeding
//! them IS the fixture: `field`, `flowField`, `tempField`, `rainField`,
//! `civWays`, `_riverNet`, `GW`/`GH`, `state.*`, and the five memos
//! `currentWaterBodies`, `currentFloodField`, `currentCarryingCapacity`,
//! `currentResourcePotentials`, `buildBiomeRaster`.
//!
//! Run live in the reference and never stubbed -- the Rust side has its own
//! port of each, so a disagreement in any of them surfaces here:
//! `chamferDist` (through `_civCoastDistField`), `slopeAt`, `gradAt`,
//! `traceRiverPolylines` (through `_civRiverPolylines`),
//! `_civPlaceConnectedRoads`, `_civPlaceResourceContext`,
//! `_civPlaceDefensibility`, `_umInferWalls`/`_umWallSpec`,
//! `riverFlowThresh`, and every `_um*` under test.
//!
//! # Two worlds, because every radius here is `max(floor, GW/k)`
//!
//! A single small grid reaches only the floors, and three constants would be
//! unobservable:
//!
//! | | Small 96x64 / 800 km | Large 448x288 / 190 km |
//! |---|---|---|
//! | `_umSiteProfile` `defR = max(4, round(GW/70))` | 4 (floor) | 6 |
//! | `_umOreBearing` `R = max(2, round(GW/64))` | 2 (floor) | 7 |
